To create or replace a trigger in PostgreSQL, you can use the `DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS` statement followed by the `CREATE TRIGGER` statement. This is because there is no direct equivalent to Oracle's "CREATE OR REPLACE TRIGGER" command.

Here is an example:

```sql
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS my_trigger ON my_table;
CREATE TRIGGER my_trigger
  AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON my_table
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE PROCEDURE my_function();
```

As for why there isn't a "CREATE OR REPLACE TRIGGER" command in PostgreSQL, it's likely because the designers of the language chose to follow a more explicit and safe approach. By requiring you to drop the trigger before creating a new one with the same name, you are forced to think carefully about what you're doing and avoid accidentally overwriting existing triggers.

Regarding your question about whether such a command is planned for PostgreSQL, I couldn't find any information on upcoming changes or additions to the `CREATE TRIGGER` statement that would include a "REPLACE" option. However, it's always possible that future versions of PostgreSQL may introduce new features or syntax changes.

In terms of best practices, it's generally recommended to use transactions when modifying triggers, as this ensures that either all changes are committed or none are, maintaining data consistency. You can wrap your `DROP TRIGGER` and `CREATE TRIGGER` statements in a transaction like so:

```sql
BEGIN;
DROP TRIGGER IF EXISTS my_trigger ON my_table;
CREATE TRIGGER my_trigger
  AFTER INSERT OR UPDATE OR DELETE ON my_table
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE PROCEDURE my_function();
COMMIT;
```

This way, if anything goes wrong during the creation of the trigger (e.g., due to a syntax error), the transaction will be rolled back and your original trigger will still exist.

The issue is that SQL Server doesn't allow creating views after dropping them, even if you use an `IF EXISTS` clause.

To fix this, you can use dynamic SQL to drop and create the view in one statement:

```sql
IF EXISTS (SELECT * FROM sys.views WHERE name = 'view1' AND type = 'V')
BEGIN
    DROP VIEW view1;
END;

CREATE VIEW view1 AS (...);
```

Alternatively, you can also use a stored procedure or a batch script to drop and recreate the view.

