You can define a composite primary key using the `@EmbeddedId` annotation on an entity class, and then use the `@Column` annotation to specify the individual columns that make up the primary key.

Here is an example:
```java
@Entity
public class MyEntity {
    @EmbeddedId
    private MyKey id;

    // other fields and methods...
}

@Embeddable
public class MyKey implements Serializable {
    @Column(name = "column1")
    private String column1;

    @Column(name = "column2")
    private String column2;

    @Column(name = "column3")
    private String column3;
}
```
In this example, `MyEntity` has a composite primary key made up of three columns: `column1`, `column2`, and `column3`. The `@EmbeddedId` annotation on the `id` field indicates that it is an embedded ID.

Alternatively, you can use the `@IdClass` annotation to define a separate class for the composite primary key.
```java
@Entity
public class MyEntity {
    @IdClass(MyKey.class)
    private MyKey id;

    // other fields and methods...
}

public class MyKey implements Serializable {
    private String column1;
    private String column2;
    private String column3;
}
```
In this case, you would need to specify the individual columns that make up the primary key using the `@Column` annotation on each field in the `MyKey` class.

Note that when using a composite primary key, you will need to use the `@EmbeddedId` or `@IdClass` annotations to indicate which fields make up the primary key.

