To alter the length of an `nvarchar` column without dropping it, you can use the following syntax:

```sql
ALTER TABLE Post ALTER COLUMN Body nvarchar(MAX);
```

However, if you want to specify a maximum length less than `MAX`, you will get the error message you mentioned because SQL Server does not support increasing the size of an `nvarchar` column from less than 4000 bytes to a size equal to or greater than 4000 bytes using in-place `ALTER TABLE`.

In this case, you can use the following syntax with `ALGORITHM=COPY`, which will create a new table with the updated column length and then drop the old table:

```sql
SELECT * INTO PostTemp FROM Post;
DROP TABLE Post;

EXEC sp_rename 'PostTemp', 'Post';

-- Now you can alter the column length as needed
ALTER TABLE Post ALTER COLUMN Body nvarchar(8000) NOT NULL;
```

Alternatively, if your database is running SQL Server 2017 or later, you can use the `MODIFY COLUMN` statement with `ALGORITHM=INPLACE`, which will modify the table in-place without creating a temporary copy:

```sql
ALTER TABLE Post MODIFY COLUMN Body nvarchar(8000) NOT NULL;
```

However, this syntax only works for increasing the length of an existing column, not decreasing it.

