**2. Solution: Check for Existence Before Dropping**

The most reliable approach is to explicitly check if the column exists before attempting to drop it.  Here's how you can do this within a Laravel migration:

```php
<?php

use Illuminate\Database\Migrations\Migration;
use Illuminate\Database\Schema\Blueprint;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Schema;

class YourMigration extends Migration
{
    /**
     * Run the migrations.
     *
     * @return void
     */
    public function up()
    {
        $column1 = 'column_name_1';
        $column2 = 'column_name_2';

        if (Schema::hasColumn('your_table', $column1)) {
            Schema::dropColumn('your_table', $column1);
        }

        if (Schema::hasColumn('your_table', $column2)) {
            Schema::dropColumn('your_table', $column2);
        }
